3. Learning phase space dynamics
Generative modeling by estimating gradients of the data distribution (also known as score-based generative models or diffusion models) is a powerful framework that could be very well-suited for investigating complex systems like coupled Josephson junctions. The key idea is to learn the score function, i.e., the gradient of the log probability density ∇x log p(x), where for our junction system as in (2.12)-(2.13), x = [φ1, φ2, φ ̇1, φ ̇2] represents the state vector. We could train score-based models to capture the complex joint distribution of junction states. Instead of solving the coupled equations (2.12)-(2.13) directly, we collect trajectory data from simulations, train a score-based model on the joint distribution of states and generate new trajectories that respect the underlying physics. The score function ∇ log p(φ1, φ2, φ ̇1, φ ̇2) would capture how likely the system is to transition between different dynamical regimes. It implicitly learn the coupling terms and thermal noise characteristics. The fluctuation-dissipation theorem relates thermal noise to damping. Score-based models could separate deterministic dynamics from stochastic components, characterize noise correlations between junctions and identify non-thermal noise sources. Implementation framework
